Protein glycosylation, as one of the most structurally complex forms of post-translational modification, plays a vital role in regulating numerous biological processes. Aberrant glycosylation has been found to be closely associated with a variety of diseases including cancers.1 Therefore, large-scale characterization of glycoproteins and precise analysis of specific glycosylation sites are crucial for improving timeliness and efficiency of clinical screening and therapy.2 Currently, bottom-up proteomics is a widely chosen method for glycoproteomics research. Peptides, resulting from proteins, are analyzed by mass spectrometry (MS) to create sequence tags for database searches.3 Unfortunately, MS-based analytical techniques present poor glycopeptides detection efficiency due to the inherent defects (low abundance and ionization efficiency of glycopeptides, heterogeneity of glycan) and the external distractors (high abundance of non-glycopeptides, proteins, and salts).4 Thus before MS analysis, developing a way for efficiently capturing glycopeptides is critical for glycoproteomics research.
Among the glycopeptides enrichment approaches, hydrophilic interaction liquid chromatography (HILIC) receives favor because of its simple operating process, good biocompatibility, and high reproducibility.5 This enrichment method mainly focuses on introducing hydrophilic groups into materials to form hydrophilic interaction with the glycan structure of glycopeptides. Due to the unbiased nature of the hydrophilic groups, hydrophilic materials can capture more glycopeptides for biochemical analysis.6 To date, a load of compounds with hydrophilic groups have been developed for glycopeptides enrichment, such as $\mathrm{GO}/\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{PEG}$ composite, glucose-6-phosphate-functionalized magnetic microspheres, etc.7-11 However, these composites appeared to possess unsatisfactory efficiencies for enriching glycopeptides in human serum, possibly ascribing to the competitive adsorption of nonglyco-peptides and -proteins.7-9 In view of the size and hydrophilicity of glycopeptides, materials with suitable channels (size-exclusion) as well as strong hydrophilic pore surface (hydrophilic interaction) are preferred.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which consist of organic ligands and metal ions, feature facile preparation, adjustable pore sizes, and modifiable functional groups compared to conventional porous materials.12 Hydrophilic MOFs, in which the organic ligands contain carboxyl groups or amino groups, are good candidates for glycopeptides enrichment. However, the two main factors which limit their application are the low hydrolytic stability and the small microporous channels. On the one hand, water has been reported to attack the metal connectors of MOFs, replacing the ligands, and causing phase changes and material decomposition.13, 14  For practical applications, the stability of MOFs is important, as high reusability and recyclability lead to cost reductions. On the other hand, a handful of MOFs, such as MIL-101(Cr) and UiO-66, have been reported to be stable in acidic aqueous solution and capable of capturing glycopeptides. For example, MIL-101(Cr)- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ enriched 116 glycopeptides corresponding to 42 glycoproteins in $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ human serum,15 its maltose functionalized structure enriched 111 glycopeptides to 65 glycoproteins in ${5\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ human serum,16 and glutathione functionalized magnetic UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ enriched 273 glycopeptides to 94 glycoproteins in $2~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ human serum.17 However, their capture efficiencies need to be improved. The intrinsic microporous channels of MOFs probably hinder the diffusion of glycopeptides.15-18 Therefore, ensuring the stability of MOFs while expanding the pores of materials for better performance is of significant importance for glycopeptides enrichment.
Herein, we reported a facile strategy to construct hydrophilic mesoporous MOF (Ce-MOF $@$ PA) by etching and simultaneously functionalizing the Ce-MOF stabilized with PVP. Cerium has some advantages in terms of enrichment;19-21 however, reports of Ce-MOFs for enrichment are rare. We infer that low hydrolytic stability and small microporous channels limit their application, so a hydrophilic Ce-MOF was selected as the model MOF for modification.21, 22 Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), as the stabilizer and capping agent, can form strong bonds with metal ions in MOF, sterically shielding most hydrolytically vulnerable M-O bonds and enhancing the hydrolytic stability of MOF.13, 23, 24 Therefore, the above PVPassisted MOF can be stably existed in aqueous solution. Subsequently, phytic acid (PA) was used as both etching agents and surface functionalization to further construct hydrophilic mesoporous MOF. As an organic acid, PA molecules released free protons capable of etching the framework of MOF while PA attached to the MOF surface, protecting the external parts from further etching.25 Besides, the PA molecule, composed of six phosphate groups, could enhance the hydrophilicity of MOF to reduce the non-specific adsorption. Owing to the expanded pore channels and excellent hydrophilicity, Ce-MOF@PA effectively and selectively captured 422 glycopeptides from 155 glycoproteins in human serum $(2\upmu\mathrm{L})$ .
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials and Chemicals. Cerium (III) nitrate hexahydrate (Ce $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , 99.99 wt. $\%$ ) was purchased from Sun Chemical Technology Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). Phytic acid (PA, 70 wt. $\%$ in water), Benzene-1, 3, 5-tricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ , poly (vinylpyrrolidone) (PVP, $\mathrm{MW}=40000\mathrm{\Omega}$ were produced by Aladdin Industrial Corporation (Shanghai, China). Analytical grade bovine serum albumin (BSA), Human serum immunoglobulin G (IgG), 2, 5-dihydroxybenzoic acid (DHB), iodoacetamide (IAA), dithiothreitol (DTT), urea (99.5 wt. $\%$ ) and trypsin were acquired from Sigma-Aldrich (St. Louis, MO). Commercial HILIC materials (Chromatorex SMB, $10{-}20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ were purchased from Fuji Silysia Chemical, Ltd. (Aichi, Japan). PNGase F (Glycerol-free, Recombinant) was bought from New England Biolabs (Ipswich, MA). All the above chemicals were analytical reagent and used as purchased without any further purification. Water used in this study was purified by using the Milli-Q system (Millipore, Bedford, MA). Healthy human serum samples were supplied by Shanghai No.6 People Hospital in accordance with standard clinical procedures.
Sample Preparation. PVP-assisted Ce-MOF was synthesized and improved based on the synthesis procedure of microporous Ce-BTC materials.22, 26 In detail, $\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ 1 $\mathrm{174mg}$ , $0.4\mathrm{~mmol}$ ) and PVP $(1.4~\mathrm{g},~0.035~\mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in a DMF-ethanol mixed solution ( $\mathrm{\Delta}64\mathrm{\mL}$ , $\mathbf{V}/\mathbf{V}=5{:}3$ ) and stirred vigorously for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . After addition of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ $(31.5~\mathrm{mg},0.15~\mathrm{mmol})$ , the mixture was stirred for a further 20 min and then transferred to a $100\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave for heating to $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After centrifugation, the precipitate was dispersed in the solution of PA $(1.0\ \mathrm{g})$ and DMF $\mathrm{50~mL}$ ) under magnetic stirring for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ . The resulting product was purified with methanol several times followed by drying in air at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ .
For comparison, microporous Ce-BTC materials (without adding PVP) were synthesized as described in previous studies.26 Briefly, a mixture of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ ( $\mathrm{16~mg}$ , $75\mathrm{\textmumol})$ , $\mathrm{Ce(NO_{3})_{3}{\bullet}6H_{2}O}$ $(87\ \mathrm{~mg},\ 0.2\ \mathrm{~mmol})$ , dimethylformamide (DMF; $20\mathrm{mL}$ ) and ethanol ( $12\mathrm{mL}$ ) was placed in a $50\mathrm{mL}$ glass reactor and stirred for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The resulting precipitate was rinsed several times with acetone and then dried in air at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ .
Characterization. The morphology and size of synthesized materials were characterized by S-3400N scanning electron microscope (SEM; Hitachi, Japan) and JEM-1400 transmission electron microscope (TEM; JEOL, Japan). The elemental mapping patterns were obtained through an XM-2 Energy Dispersive Spectrometer (EDAX, USA) equipped with a Tecnai G2 F30 S-TWIN Field Emission Transmission Electron Microscope (FEI, USA). Zeta potentials of the materials in the ultrapure water were measured at room temperature with a Nano-ZS90 instrument (Malvern, UK). The crystallinity of the products was determined by a D/MAX-2550 rotating anode Xray powder diffractometer (XRD; Rigaku International Co, Japan). The patterns were recorded using copper radiation ( $\mathrm{Cu}$ $\mathrm{K}\bar{\alpha},\lambda{=}1.540\bar{5}\mathrm{\AA};$ with a fixed monochromator at $40\mathrm{kV}$ and 100 mA at a scanning speed of $2^{\circ}$ (2θ) $\mathrm{min^{-1}}$ . Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy characterization was performed with a Nicolet 6700 using KBr particles (Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA) to confirm the presence of phosphate groups. Elemental analysis of products was measured by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) operated on ESCALAB250Xi (Thermo-Fisher Co, USA). The Brunauer-EmmettTeller (BET) specific surface areas of the materials were measured by Micromeritics ASAP 2020 V4.02 (V4.02J).
Tryptic Digestion of Standard Glycoproteins and Human Serum. Samples of IgG tryptic digests and healthy human serum tryptic digests were prepared based on the previous work.21 A total of $1\mathrm{mg}$ of IgG and $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{NH_{4}H C O_{3}}$ buffer 1 $50~\mathrm{mM}$ , $\mathrm{pH}8.3$ ) containing $8\mathrm{M}$ urea were blended by shaking to obtain a protein solution. The protein solution was incubated with ${5}\upmu\mathrm{L}$ DTT $(200\mathrm{mM})$ for $45\mathrm{min}$ at $56^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then alkylated by $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ IAA $(200~\mathrm{mM})$ in the dark for $30~\mathrm{min}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Prior to digestion, $\mathrm{NH_{4}H C O_{3}}$ buffer $(50~\mathrm{mM})$ was added into the solution to a final protein concentration of $1\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}}$ . Trypsin was added (enzyme/substrate $=1/25$ , w/w) into the protein solution and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $17\mathrm{~h~}$ for digestion. Finally, digestion was terminated by the addition of ${5}\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of pure FA.
The tryptic digestion procedure of human serum was identical to that of IgG. Briefly, $2~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ supernatant of serum sample was denatured with $18\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{NH_{4}H C O_{3}}$ buffer $(50\mathrm{mM})$ containing $^{8\mathrm{M}}$ urea, then the mixture was treated by a reductive alkylation reaction with DTT and IAA. The acquired solution, $16~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of ammonium bicarbonate $(50~\mathrm{~mM})$ and trypsin (enzyme/substrate $=1/25$ , w/w) were blended homogeneously for $17\mathrm{h}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After incubation, $1~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of pure FA was added to terminate the digestion. All of the samples were lyophilized and stored at $-80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for further use.
Protocol of the Enrichment Process. Typically, $2\mathrm{mg}$ of CeMOF@PA mesoporous materials were first cleaned twice with $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of loading buffer $\mathrm{(ACN/H_{2}O/T F A=90/9/1,v/v/v}$ ) and then diluted with $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of loading buffer to $2~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ CeMOF@PA suspension. $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of this suspension was added into $190~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of loading buffer containing $6~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ of $\mathrm{IgG}$ digests, and vigorously shaken at room temperature for $30\mathrm{min}$ . After being washed three times with $100~\mathrm{\textmuL}$ loading buffer, the nanomaterials were eluted with $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $1\%$ TFA aqueous solution. Finally, the obtained eluent contained the captured glycopeptides. $0.5~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the resulting peptides solution was deposited on a MALDI plate and allowed to dry. Then $0.5~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of matrix solution (DHB, 20 mg $\mathrm{mL^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{ACN/H_{2}O/H_{3}P O_{4}}\mathrm{=}70{\cdot}29{\cdot}1$ , v/v/v) was added together and allowed to dry and analyzed by matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization time-of-flight mass spectrometry (MALDI-TOF MS).
For the glycopeptides enrichment of human serum digests, 2 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of lyophilized serum digests was dissolved in $500~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of loading buffer and incubated with $400~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathsf{C e-M O F}@\mathsf{P A}$ or commercial HILIC materials for $30~\mathrm{\min}$ . After that, the materials were washed three times with ${500\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ washing buffer and eluted twice with $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $1\%$ aqueous TFA solution for $20\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\operatorname{min}}$ . The collected eluent was lyophilized and deglycosylated for further nano-liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (nano-LC-MS/MS) analysis.
Deglycosylation of Glycopeptides by PNGase F. To deglycosylate glycopeptides extracted from the serum digests, a mixture of the lyophilized peptides eluent, $17~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , 2 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 10X GlycoBuffer 2 and $1\upmu\mathrm{L}$ PNGase F was incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $16\mathrm{h}$ and lyophilized for nano-LC-MS/MS analysis.
MALDI-TOF MS Analysis. All MALDI-TOF-MS measurements were operated in a reflection positive mode with an Nd-YAG laser at $355\mathrm{nm}$ on an AB Sciex 4800 Plus MALDITOF mass spectrometer (AB Sciex, USA). The laser pulse frequency was $200\mathrm{Hz}$ and the acceleration voltage was $20\mathrm{kV}$ .
Nano-LC-MS/MS Analysis. The nano-LC-MS/MS analysis was performed on a Thermo EASY-nLC 1000 Nano HPLC System (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Bremen, Germany) connected to a quadrupole-Orbitrap mass spectrometer (QExactive plus, Thermo Fisher Scientific, Bremen, Germany) with an online nano-electrospray ion source. Firstly, three lyophilized deglycosylated peptides samples were redissolved with $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of solvent A, respectively, (A: water with $0.1\%$ formic acid; B: ACN with $0.1\%$ formic acid) and each $1\ \upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the peptides sample was loaded onto a C18 analytical column (Thermo Scientific Acclaim PepMap C18, $50\upmu\mathrm{m}\upkappa15\mathrm{cm}$ . Then a $60\mathrm{min}$ elution gradient (3 min, 3- $8\%$ B; $45~\mathrm{{min}}$ , $8-28\%$ B; $54~\mathrm{min}$ , $28-38\%$ B; $56~\mathrm{min}$ , $38\mathrm{-}100\%$ B; $58~\mathrm{min}$ , $100\%$ B; $58.5\ \mathrm{min}$ , $100\text{\textperthousand}$ B and $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , $3\%$ B) was employed to separate and identify the peptides. Finally, the column was re-equilibrated with constant flow rate at $300~\mathrm{nL}$ $\mathrm{min^{-1}}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . The column temperature and electrospray voltage were set to $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $1.9\ \mathrm{kV}$ , respectively. The QExactive mass spectrometer was operated in data-dependent MS or MS/MS acquisitions mode and Survey full-scan MS range was from $\mathrm{m}/\mathrm{z}350$ to 1600 with a mass resolution of 70 K. Subsequently, the 20 most intense parent ions with charge states $2+$ , $^{3+}$ , and $^{4+}$ were selected for high energy collisional dissociation (HCD) MS/MS scans with a resolution of $17.5\mathrm{K}$ . For MS, the automatic gain control (AGC) was set to 100 0000 ions and the maximum accumulation times was $50~\mathrm{ms}$ . For MS/MS, the AGC target was set to $100\ 000$ ions and the maximum accumulation times was $45~\mathrm{ms}$ . Besides, precursor ions were activated with $28\%$ normalized collision energy with the isolation window at $2{\mathrm{m/z}}$ . In all cases, one microscan was used as $30~\mathrm{s}$ dynamic exclusion.
Database Retrieval and Data Analysis. All tandem mass spectral data were obtained from the human UniProtKB/SwissProt database and extracted using the Sequest HT search engine in Thermo Scientific Proteome Discoverer software version 2.2. The Q-Exactive plus instrument was used to generate raw files with a ${50}\mathrm{{mmu}}$ fragment mass tolerance and a $10\mathrm{ppm}$ precursor mass tolerance. The trypsin was used for enzyme specificity and up to two missed cleavages were allowed in detecting. The percolator algorithm was used to control a peptide level false discovery rate (FDR) of less than $1\%$ . The carbamidomethyl (C) on cysteine was specified as a fixed modification. Oxidation on methionine (M) and Deamidation on asparagine (N) were variably modified. Only peptides which were conformed to the consensus sequence (N-X-S/T/C, X! $\mathbf{\Lambda}=\mathbf{P}$ ) were considered to be highly reliable glycopeptides.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Material Characterization. Figure 1a illustrates the preparation of Ce-MOF@PA through a facile two-step route and the innovative application in the glycoproteomics analysis. Firstly, Ce-MOF substrate was synthesized through introducing PVP into MOF synthetic solution. During the synthesis of MOF, PVP molecules could protect hydrolytically susceptible coordination backbones due to the strong coordination interaction between the metal ion sites in MOF and the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ groups in PVP.27  Subsequently, since the MOF structure was rich in positively charged metal ions,28 the substrate Ce-MOF exhibited a positive Zeta potential of around $+21.7\ \mathrm{mV}$ and rapidly assembled with negative phosphate groups of PA via electrostatic interaction, causing the Zeta potential of CeMOF@PA reduced to $-20.8\mathrm{mV}$ .
Morphology and size of the products were visualized by using SEM and TEM, and the results are shown in Figure 2. Compared with the rod structure of Ce-BTC (Figure 2a, d), CeMOF was a uniform porous acicular crystal with an average side length of around $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure 2b, e). After treatment with PA, Ce-MOF was modified with PA molecules on the surface, and free $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ ions from PA molecules penetrated into the MOF for etching, resulting in the appearance of mesoporous structure of Ce-MOF@PA (Figure 2f). The mesoporosity of Ce$\mathbf{MOF}@\mathbf{PA}$ $(3.5\ \mathrm{nm})$ was confirmed by the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorptiondesorption isotherms, and the microporosity of Ce-BTC (0.5 nm) was also investigated. Detailed information on surface area measurement and pore size distribution is shown in Figure S1. The EDS element mapping of a single crystal revealed a uniform distribution of P, C, N, O and Ce elements on CeMOF@PA (Figure 2g).
XPS characterization was performed to further evaluate the chemical composition of the products and the valence state of P element in Ce-MOF@PA. Comparing the two survey spectra of Ce-MOF (Figure 3a) and Ce-MOF@PA (Figure 3b), CeMOF@PA had extra peaks belonging to the characteristic $\mathrm{~P~}2\mathrm{s}$ and P 2p peaks at 191 and $134~\mathrm{eV}$ ,29 confirming the existence of PA in Ce-MOF $\ @\mathrm{PA}$ . The fitted sub-peaks of $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}$ could be assigned as the core levels of P atoms in phosphate at $133.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ and P-O-Ce at $134.1~\mathrm{eV},\mathrm{^{30}}$ respectively, confirming the successful grafting of PA (Figure 3c). FT-IR spectra (Figure 3d) of Ce-MOF $\ @\mathrm{PA}$ showed the appearance of additional peaks at 1162, 1068 and $994~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , which were attributed to the stretching vibrations of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ , P-O-C and P-O bonds of $\mathrm{-PO}_{4}{}^{3.}$ - group, respectively. The peak appearing around $516~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ corresponded
to the deformation vibration of the $-\mathrm{PO}_{4}{}^{3-}$ group from PA molecule. 29, 31 The above results indicated that the PA had been successfully linked to the substrate surface.
The stability of Ce-BTC, Ce-MOF and Ce-MOF@PA in acidic aqueous solution was displayed in Figure S2 and evaluated using XRD. Compared with the dissolution of CeBTC, both Ce-MOF and Ce-MOF@PA were stable in the $1\%$ TFA aqueous solution. XRD results also confirmed the stability of Ce-MOF@PA. After 7 days of exposure to TFA aqueous solution in $\mathrm{pH}=1$ , the characteristic XRD peaks of Ce$\mathbf{MOF}@\mathbf{PA}$ did not change significantly (Figure S3). These results indicated that modification of PVP and PA improved the stability of MOF. PVP molecules could protect the hydrolytically susceptible M-O bonds in MOF to augment the hydrolytic stability of MOFs. PA molecules released free protons capable of etching the framework of MOF, while their relatively large molecular size allowed them to block the pores of the MOF, thereby protecting the MOF from complete collapse.25, 31 The PA molecules attached to the MOF were able to block the exposed surface of the MOF and protect the external components from further etching.25 In addition, the peaks of Ce-MOF@PA coincided with the main peaks of CeBTC, confirming the successful synthesis of MOF.
Enrichment of Glycopeptides from Standard Sample. In order to assess the performance of Ce-MOF@PA in glycopeptides enrichment, tryptic digests of IgG glycoprotein were selected as a standard sample for detection. The flow chart of Ce-MOF@PA enriched glycopeptides is demonstrated in Figure 1a. Generally, HILIC-based materials were incubated with IgG tryptic digests in the loading buffer $\mathrm{{'ACN/H_{2}O/T F A}}$ $\mathbf{\tau}=90/9/1,\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v})$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ and then washed with loading buffer three times. After washed, the materials were eluted with $1\%$ TFA aqueous solution to obtain the captured glycopeptides in elution, and this eluate was analyzed by MALDI-TOF MS. For comparison, the $\mathrm{IgG}$ digests without enrichment were analyzed using MALDI-TOF MS. As shown in Figure 4a, only one glycopeptide with low MS intensity was detected prior to enrichment, and non-glycopeptides dominated the MS spectrum, whereas, after captured with Ce-MOF@PA, 39 noninterfering glycopeptides (details of the identified glycopeptides are listed in
Table S1) were clearly detected and the signal intensity of glycopeptides was significantly enhanced (Figure 4c). The amount of identified glycopeptides from IgG digests exceeded those of many previously reported hydrophilic materials, such as $\mathrm{GO}/\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{PEG}$ materials (20),9 zwitterionic polymercoated core-shell magnetic nanoparticles (28),32 and Glucose-6- Phosphate-Functionalized Magnetic Microspheres (14),7 indicating the outstanding enrichment performance of CeMOF@PA nanocomposites. The high enrichment efficiency of Ce-MOF@PA could be attributed to their channel structure, which provided a suitable pore size allowing the diffusion of glycopeptides.33 Thereafter, Ce-MOF@PA captured their terminal glycan through a strong hydrophilic interaction.
Moreover, to investigate the role of PA and PVP in enrichment, Ce-BTC and Ce-MOF were also used to capture glycopeptides under the same conditions. Unfortunately, CeBTC was directly dissolved in the loading buffer, resulting in the loss of enrichment ability. For Ce-MOF, the introduction of PVP improved the resistance of MOF against water and acidic media,23, 27 which made Ce-MOF have potential in the enrichment process. However, the enrichment performance of Ce-MOF was not so satisfactory, both the number (24) and the signal intensity of detected glycopeptides were inferior to that of Ce-MOF@PA. And due to the weak hydrophilicity of CeMOF, non-glycopeptides with nonspecific adsorption of $\mathrm{m}/\mathrm{z}$ 2544 and 2584 were also detected (Figure 4b).
The enrichment selectivity of Ce-MOF and Ce-MOF@PA toward glycopeptides was evaluated using IgG digests with different molar ratios of BSA digests. When the concentration of BSA was 200 times higher than that of IgG digests, in the absence of enrichment, no glycopeptide was observed due to the interference with BSA tryptic digests (Figure S4). After the enrichment with Ce-MOF, 8 low-intensity glycopeptides were detected, and high-intensity non-glycopeptide peaks were dominant in the range of $\mathrm{m}/z\ 3200$ to $\mathrm{m}/z\ 3500$ (Figure 5a). However, after enrichment with $\mathbf{Ce-MOF}@\mathbf{PA}$ , 18 glycopeptides could be distinctly identified and the intensity of glycopeptides dramatically increased (Figure 5b). Even though the ratio of BSA digests: IgG digests increased to 500: 1, Ce$\mathbf{MOF}@\mathbf{PA}$ still exhibited a good selective enrichment ability towards glycopeptides in which 12 glycopeptides were recognized (Figure S5). Also, the recovery yield of CeMOF@PA and Ce-MOF was studied by stable isotope dimethyl labeling technique with IgG digests $(3~\upmu\mathrm{g})$ , and the results obtained were $93.7\%\pm\ 6.4\%$ by using $\mathbf{Ce-MOF}@\mathbf{PA}$ and $69.5\%\pm\ 13.4\%$ by using Ce-MOF (Figure 5c and S6, Table S2). The above results indicated that the PA component on Ce$\mathbf{MOF}@\mathbf{PA}$ showed a significant improvement in selectivity and recovery yield of glycopeptides enrichment. This may be due to the fact that the PA layer contains a large number of phosphate groups and hydroxyl groups, which improved the hydrophilicity of MOF and thus increased its enrichment efficiency.34
Next, Ce-MOF and Ce-MOF@PA were recycled by washing with loading buffer to further examine the stability and reusability of the materials. As revealed in Figure S7, after reuse of Ce-MOF twice, the enrichment capability was reduced ascribed to the gradual degradation of Ce-MOF in acidic aqueous solution. However, for Ce-MOF@PA, the MS spectrum of glycopeptides after repeated enrichment five times, whether the signal intensity or the number of identified glycopeptides, was almost identical to that of the first use (Figure 5d-f), suggesting that Ce-MOF@PA owned splendid recyclability and chemical stability in the glycopeptides enrichment. The above results indicated that the existence of PA not only increased the hydrophilicity of Ce-MOF,35 but also enhanced the stability by strong chelation between PA and the abundant coordination unsaturated metal sites in MOF.23, 31, 36
The prominent feature of Ce-MOF $@$ PA was appropriate pore size for peptides, which could provide suitable hydrophilic channels to enrich glycopeptides while excluding large-size proteins. Accordingly, a mixture of glycopeptides and largesized proteins (BSA,37 non-glycoprotein, $5\times7\times7\mathrm{nm}$ , $66\mathrm{kDa}$ , and IgG,38 glycoprotein, $7\times19\times7\mathrm{nm}$ , $150\mathrm{kDa}$ ) was employed to detect the size exclusion effect of Ce-MOF $\ @\mathrm{PA}$ (Figure 6). When the molar ratio of the mixture sharply increased to 1:1000:1000 (IgG tryptic digests: IgG: BSA), before enrichment, none of the glycopeptides peaks were observed due to the highly abundant proteins of BSA and IgG. However, after treatment with Ce-MOF@PA, 7 glycopeptides peaks were detected clearly and the proteins peaks disappeared, indicating that the competitive adsorption of proteins was well suppressed by the size exclusion effect.
A series of diluted IgG digests (5, 2, and $1\ \mathrm{fmol\\upmuL^{-1}},$ ) were used to explore the enrichment sensitivity of Ce-MOF@PA for glycopeptides. As shown in Figure S8, even when the concentration of IgG digests was low as 1 fmol $\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , 3 glycopeptides peaks could still be identified. Different amounts of Ce-MOF $@$ PA and IgG digests $(6~\upmu\mathrm{g})$ were mixed to investigate the enrichment capacity of the material. As illustrated in Figure S9, it can be calculated that the loading capacity of Ce-MOF@PA for glycopeptides is up to $300\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-}$ 1
Enrichment of Glycopeptides from Tryptic Digests of Human Serum. Reversible protein glycosylation is involved in many cellular functions, so large-scale characterization of glycoproteins and precise analysis of specific glycosylation sites in human serum can provide potential therapeutic options for diseases. Unfortunately, glycopeptides from biosamples are hard to analyze directly due to interferences such as salts, proteins, etc. Encouraged by the results of the above mimic biosamples, Ce-MOF@PA was further employed to large-scale characterization of glycopeptides from human serum. By taking advantage to nano-LC-MS/MS analysis, a total of 422 glycopeptides assigned to 155 glycoproteins (Details of the detected glycopeptides are shown in Table S3) were identified in three parallel replicates of $2~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ human serum digests. For comparison, the commercial HILIC material was also used to capture
glycopeptides under the same conditions, and 110 glycopeptides corresponding to 60 glycoproteins were identified (Table S4). Obviously, Ce-MOF@PA exhibited better performance than commercial HILIC (Figure 7). Furthermore, several research groups have reported various hydrophilic organic frameworks used in the enrichment of glycopeptides from human serum.39 A brief comparison of representative hydrophilic organic frameworks is listed in Table S5. Compared with other functionalized hydrophilic organic frameworks, Ce-MOF@PA also reveals satisfactory results. Hence, Ce-MOF@PA is promising in glycoproteomics research.
## CONCLUSIONS
The main feature of this work is the development of a facile way to create the hydrophilic mesoporous MOF via synergistic etching and surface functionalization. The great stability of the modified MOF was demonstrated, and the etching process well controlled the pore size suitable for glycopeptides enrichment. The surface modification provided the mesoporous MOFs with more hydrophilic groups, leading to the excellent enrichment performance of Ce-MOF@PA in glycopeptides enrichment. We expect that the PVP- and PA-assisted engineering strategy can be applied to other MOFs, endowing them with unique performances.
